# Copy to `.env` and fill in for live LLM tests.
#
# A cwd `.env` is for PROVIDER CREDENTIALS, plus a short list of
# PROVIDER-CONFIGURATION names (ADR-0203). aelix reads this file before it knows
# whether it trusts the directory, so a `.env` that came with a repo you cloned
# would otherwise be able to spawn processes, relocate aelix's global settings
# file and redirect API traffic.
#
# Loaded:
#   * names ending in `_API_KEY` / `_KEY` / `_TOKEN` / `_SECRET` / `_PASSWORD`
#     — MINUS the names `_DOTENV_NEVER` subtracts first, which is a real
#     subtraction and not a formality: it removes twelve prefixes
#     (`AELIX_`, `PI_`, `PYTHON_`, `LD_`, `BASH_`, `NODE_`, `PERL_`, `RUBY_`,
#     `GIT_`, `SSH_`, `NPM_`, `npm_`) and ten substrings (`URL`, `PATH`, `DIR`,
#     `HOME`, `SHELL`, `PROXY`, `OPTS`, …), so `NPM_TOKEN`, `NODE_AUTH_TOKEN`,
#     `GIT_TOKEN`, `SSH_KEY` and `PROXY_PASSWORD` do NOT load even though they
#     end in a credential suffix — see the note below
#   * six provider-configuration names — `GOOGLE_CLOUD_PROJECT`,
#     `GCLOUD_PROJECT`, `GOOGLE_CLOUD_LOCATION`, `CLOUDFLARE_ACCOUNT_ID`,
#     `CLOUDFLARE_GATEWAY_ID`, `OPENROUTER_DEFAULT_MODEL` — and only if the VALUE
#     is a plain name/id, because a Vertex location containing `/`, `@` or `:`
#     would move requests off googleapis.com, and a Cloudflare id containing
#     `/`, `?`, `#` or `..` would rewrite the request path under Cloudflare's
#     own host.
# Everything else is ignored, with a notice on stderr naming it.
#
# Five of those six are there because they decide which models you can SEE.
# Measured over the whole 1001-model catalog, exactly six environment names are
# consulted when aelix decides whether a model is runnable — those five plus
# `GOOGLE_CLOUD_API_KEY`, which the credential rule already admits. Refuse one and
# a provider's models silently disappear from `/model`, which is what happened to
# Vertex and then to both Cloudflare providers while this file was being written;
# the list is now held by a test rather than by anyone's memory.
# (`OPENROUTER_DEFAULT_MODEL` is the sixth loaded name and the odd one out: it
# chooses a model rather than revealing one.)
#
# NOT loaded, and worth naming because the failure is confusing rather than loud:
#   * credentials for OTHER tools, refused by the prefix/substring subtraction
#     above even though they look like credentials — `NPM_TOKEN`,
#     `NODE_AUTH_TOKEN`, `GIT_TOKEN`, `SSH_KEY`, `PROXY_PASSWORD`. Nothing here
#     needs them for a turn; the tools that do read them from your shell, and
#     `AELIX_DOTENV_ALLOW` opts one back in. This is the most confusing refusal
#     of the set, because the suffix rule above says a `_TOKEN` loads and for
#     these it does not.
#   * TLS trust — `SSL_CERT_FILE`, `SSL_CERT_DIR`, `REQUESTS_CA_BUNDLE`,
#     `CURL_CA_BUNDLE`, `NODE_EXTRA_CA_CERTS`. A CA bundle a repo chooses is a
#     trust store a repo chooses: measured through the real stack, setting
#     `SSL_CERT_FILE` takes the HTTP clients aelix builds from the system CA set
#     (120 anchors on the machine this was measured on) down to whatever is in
#     that one file — httpx reads the variable itself, and injecting `truststore`
#     does not change that. Behind a corporate TLS-inspecting proxy, export it in
#     your shell; aelix's own connection-error hint already tells you to.
#   * SDK-level knobs the vendor libraries read for themselves — `OPENAI_ORG_ID`,
#     `OPENAI_PROJECT_ID`, `ANTHROPIC_CUSTOM_HEADERS`, `AWS_REGION`,
#     `AZURE_OPENAI_ENDPOINT` and the like. Nothing here needs them for a working
#     turn; export them if your account does.
#   * `AELIX_CODEX_ORIGINATOR` — the attribution string the openai-codex adapter
#     sends. `AELIX_*` is never repo-settable (that is what keeps a future aelix
#     knob named `*_KEY` out of a repo's reach), and the built-in `"aelix"` is
#     the right value. Hatchable if you genuinely need it.
#   * a models.json custom provider whose declared `apiKey` env-var name is not
#     credential-SHAPED (e.g. `ACME_ENDPOINT_NAME`). Name it `*_API_KEY` and it
#     loads from here; otherwise export it or list it in `AELIX_DOTENV_ALLOW`.
#
# Configuration goes in your shell, not here:
#
#     export OPENROUTER_BASE_URL=https://my-proxy.example/v1
#
# ...or opt one key back in, from your own environment:
#
#     export AELIX_DOTENV_ALLOW=OPENROUTER_BASE_URL
#
# `AELIX_DOTENV_ALLOW` is read from the real environment only and takes no
# wildcard. Some names it can never unlock, on one rule: a project file may
# REDIRECT, but it may never make aelix EXECUTE something, and it may never
# choose where aelix's global settings and credentials live. See `_DOTENV_LOCKED`
# in `packages/aelix-coding-agent/src/aelix_coding_agent/cli/runtime_bootstrap.py`
# for the current list.
#
# Precedence — what a value in your shell does and does not beat:
#   1. a value you exported under the SAME name always wins over this file;
#   2. for a provider aelix resolves itself, a key from your shell also wins over
#      a DIFFERENT name for the same provider set here (e.g. your exported
#      ANTHROPIC_API_KEY is not silently outranked by an ANTHROPIC_OAUTH_TOKEN
#      in this file);
#   3. it is NOT universal: programs aelix runs have their own precedence. `gh`
#      prefers `GH_TOKEN` over `GITHUB_TOKEN` (measured, gh 2.88.0), so a
#      `GH_TOKEN` here does outrank a `GITHUB_TOKEN` in your shell. Export
#      `GH_TOKEN` yourself if that matters.

# OpenRouter
OPENROUTER_API_KEY=

# `OPENROUTER_BASE_URL` used to be listed here, set to the default
# `https://openrouter.ai/api/v1`. It is now ignored from `.env` — and because it
# was byte-identical to the built-in default, anyone who copied this file
# verbatim sees no behaviour change. Proxy users: export it, per above.

# Same shape for the other providers:
# ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=
# OPENAI_API_KEY=

# Google Vertex (ADC — the documented no-API-key path):
# GOOGLE_CLOUD_PROJECT=my-gcp-project
# GOOGLE_CLOUD_LOCATION=us-central1
#
# `GOOGLE_APPLICATION_CREDENTIALS` is NOT read from this file: it points at a
# full service-account identity, which a repo must not be able to choose for
# you. It is not needed for the flow above — measured, `google.auth` finds
# `application_default_credentials.json` at its well-known Cloud SDK location
# with no environment variable at all, so `gcloud auth application-default
# login` plus the two lines above works entirely from `.env`. For the
# service-account-file flow, export it in your shell.

# Cloudflare AI Gateway / Workers AI — the account and gateway ids are not
# secrets, but they ARE the `{...}` tokens in those models' base URLs, so a model
# stays hidden from `/model` until every token it carries is filled. Measured:
# with neither id set, 0 of 43; with the account id alone, the 8 Workers AI models
# appear and the 35 gateway ones do not; with both, all 43:
# CLOUDFLARE_API_KEY=
# CLOUDFLARE_ACCOUNT_ID=
# CLOUDFLARE_GATEWAY_ID=
